  "step_3": "The decision reason requires evaluating the shape of the outcome distribution (specifically right-skewness) and preferring the more right-skewed option. However, the individual does not consider distributional shape or skewness at all. Instead, the individual focuses narrowly on the probability of 'winning' (non-zero outcome) and the certainty of A. Furthermore, the individual misrepresents B’s win probability as 70% instead of 80%, suggesting a coarse, qualitative judgment rather than a detailed probabilistic analysis. The justification is consistent with a certainty effect or risk aversion, not skewness-based reasoning. There is no alignment between the information required by the decision reason (skewness) and what the individual considered (chance of any positive outcome). Similarly, the rule of preferring more right-skewed distributions is not reflected in the individual’s logic. Therefore, it is certain that the decision reason was not used.",
